(window.__CLAIMS_ADD__||function(){})([{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"193 counties with no general-enrollment MA-PD in 2027, up from 123 (KFF, 2026-10-09).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-1"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"638,000 people, 1% of Medicare. These are all beneficiaries living there, not displaced enrollees.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-2"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Alaska has no MA-PD anywhere, about 110,000 beneficiaries.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-3"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"187 of the 193 have no general-enrollment MA plan of any kind; cost plans and SNPs may exist.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-4"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"HCSC exits 498 counties, enters none.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-5"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"UnitedHealth 228 out / 9 in; Humana 62 out / 4 in. (Also Centene 359/15, Elevance 66/10, and BCBS of Michigan 177/1, which the engine's report omits.)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-6"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"KFF does not count displaced people and does not look at costs or benefits.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-7"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Comparator for scale: the average beneficiary still has 28 MA-PDs to choose from in 2027.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-8"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Humana's 2027 \"exits affect about 600,000 members\" (the peg hunt, from Insurance Business), read as members losing Advantage in the counties Humana leaves.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-9"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Humana's 2025 recapture benchmark.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-10"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"The peg hunt's \"mostly in plans rated 3.5 stars or lower\": right, but the CFO denies it is a star-ratings decision.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-11"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Scale: Humana's total MA membership June 30, 2026 (thousands), so 600,000 is about 8%.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-12"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Insurance Business: members \"have until December 7\". The non-renewal SEP runs to Feb 28, 2027 (log 254).","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-13"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"No rural/urban split of the 193 counties; Minnesota anecdote only.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-14"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"The ATI 36% \"does not say whether Medigap holders are in its comparison group\". FFS group includes Medigap (38%) and employer (37%) holders; spending counts their premiums.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-15"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"ATI/BMA: MA enrollees spent 36% (about $2,824) less in 2023 than FFS enrollees. Propensity-weighted, community-dwelling only; unweighted the gap is $3,769 (49%); observational (\"results should not be interpreted as causal\").","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-16"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"ATI/BMA: cost burden (over 20% of income on health care incl. premiums) 12% MA vs 24% FFS. Same release: 54% of MA vs 30% of FFS enrollees below 200% FPL, so MA's group is poorer and more often Medicaid-backed (ATI adjusts for dual status).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-17"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Critique: MCBS survey spending for MA enrollees cannot be checked against claims, biasing MA spending downward (KFF methodology note). Seen only in a search excerpt; both KFF pages 404'd. ATI's own brief says the survey adjusts for MA under-reporting.","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-18"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Annals 2024 switchers: +$168 out of pocket (95% CI -$133 to $469), not significant; 7,054 stayers vs 1,544 switchers. Authors' conclusion: differences \"small\" and \"did not have a consistent pattern\".","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-19"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Annals data years \"2014-2020\" (the engine, via trade press) → 2014 to 2021.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-20"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Point estimates for higher spending and burden were larger for minority and under-200%-FPL beneficiaries. Full text CF-blocked; AJMC's summary only (its sentence is garbled: \"between 200%\"). MDedge adds \"one to six more\" per 100 low-income switchers faced catastrophic burden and a 10.5% relative increase on a $1,597 baseline. Not significance-tested in what I could read.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-21"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"HAS 2025 (HRS 2008-2020; people choosing at 65-66; duals excluded): no-cancer MA enrollees spent less than TM WITHOUT a supplement at the 50th-95th percentiles ($168, $547, $1,132, $2,362).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-22"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"HAS 2025, cancer-history group (not in the engine's report): MA spent $669-$9,884 less than TM-without-supplement.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-23"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"HAS 2025: TM PLUS a supplement also cut the highest spending for the cancer group (not in the engine's report); the comparator in every HAS figure is TM with no supplement.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-24"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Cancer-history MA enrollees about 7 points less likely to skip medication for cost (vs TM without supplement). The paper says it cannot establish causation.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-25"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Cost-related problems in 2018: MA 19%, TM 15%, TM with supplement 12% (KFF, published 2021, MCBS 2018: before the Part D cap).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-26"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"30% of TM-without-supplement had cost problems, applied to \"the roughly 13%\". The 30% is 2018, when that group was about one in five TM.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-27"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"KFF 2018 (not in the engine's report): among people in fair or poor health, MA 39% vs TM 31% reported cost problems. The \"when you get sick\" cost datum.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-28"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"In 2023, 87% of TM had a supplement: Medigap 43%, employer 29%, Medicaid 14%.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-29"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"3.5 million TM enrollees (13%) had no supplement in 2023 (down from 17% in 2018).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-30"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"\"Original Medicare Parts A/B have no cap\" was never sourced. Medicare.gov states it, with the supplement exception.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-31"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"KFF: traditional Medicare is the only major form of insurance without an out-of-pocket cap (a 1988 cap was repealed a year later).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-32"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"2026 ceiling $9,250 in-network, $13,900 combined; plans may set lower.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-33"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"2027 mandatory in-network ceiling $9,850 (all plan types). Now CMS-primary: Table 3, \"FINAL CY 2027 PART C MOOP LIMITS BY PLAN TYPE\"; lower tier up to $4,450, intermediate up to $7,150.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-34"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"2027 PPO combined (in- plus out-of-network) ceiling $14,800.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-35"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"2026 enrollment-weighted average MOOP $5,421 in-network, $9,825 combined; individual plans only (21.3 million; excludes SNPs and employer plans). HMO $4,636, PPO $6,592.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-36"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"KFF 2026 (not in the engine's report): 9% of individual-plan enrollees sit at the $9,250 maximum; 13% have limits of $3,000 or less.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-37"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"No one knows how many enrollees actually hit their cap.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-38"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Medigap Plan G \"effectively caps\" TM exposure. KFF: Plan G covers everything in A/B cost sharing except the Part B deductible (it does not cover drugs, dental, vision, hearing; not checked here).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-39"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Baseline cost of TM with no supplement, 2026: Part A hospital deductible $1,736 per benefit period (can recur in a year, per Medicare.gov); Part B $283 deductible then usually 20% coinsurance.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-40"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Average MA premium projected $14.37 → $12.00 (-16.5%) for 2027; weighted across ALL MA plans incl. MA-PDs and SNPs; a CMS projection from bids.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-41"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Hearing, dental and vision offerings \"expected to remain stable\" in 2027 (CMS's expectation, not a count).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-42"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Stand-alone drug plan (the Original Medicare route's Part D) average premium projected $35.09 → $36 in 2027.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-43"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Drug portion of MA-PD premiums $11.32 → $7, after rebates (the taxpayer-funded buy-down; pass D's domain for the money).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-44"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Part B 2026 $202.90 (deductible $283); paid on BOTH routes.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-45"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Part B 2027 projected $209.50, deductible $292 (Trustees intermediate estimate; columns confirmed: premium, deductible, Part D base premium, Part D deductible, initial limit, catastrophic threshold). Not announced; CMS usually announces in November.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-46"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"KFF 2026: 75% of individual MA-PD enrollees pay no premium beyond Part B; enrollment-weighted average $15 a month; $59 among the 25% who pay one. Different universe from CMS's $14.37 (all plans incl. SNPs).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-47"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Average Plan G premium among current policyholders $194 a month in 2025 ($2,323/yr), $164 NM to $257 NY. All-plan average $244.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-48"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Not in the engine's report: Plan G premiums rose 13% in one year (2024-25) and 38% over five.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-49"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Nine states require community rating at 65+ (AR, CT, ID, MA, ME, MN, NY, VT, WA); AZ, FL, GA, MO bar attained-age rating.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-50"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"\"most other states use attained-age rating\" → most states allow any rating system.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-51"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"New Plan G applicants via one broker in 2026: median $150 at 65, $219 at 76+. The broker's own book (a commercial source), not a market average; it says so itself.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-52"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Guaranteed-issue + community-rating states (ME, NY, CT) have far fewer Plan G offerings and pricier premiums ($2,760 vs $2,497 a year). Nuance the engine's report lacks: community-rating-only states were CHEAPER ($2,290). Cross-sectional.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-53"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"2027 high-deductible Plan G/F/J deductible $3,050 (CMS, October 2026). \"Up from $2,950 in 2026\" is consistent with the 3.40% CPI step but the 2026 figure is not in any file I saved.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-54"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"2027 standard-benefit out-of-pocket threshold $2,400 (from $2,100) and deductible $700 (from $615); Table V-2, \"Defined Standard Benefit\".","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-55"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"The IRA set $2,000 for 2025 and indexes it after; the $2,400 is not a fixed statutory number.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-56"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Medicare.gov: the Part D yearly cap ($2,100 in 2026) includes the deductible and is listed for Part D generally, not by route.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-57"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Individual MA-PD sponsors put over $600 per enrollee (about $50 a month) of rebates into Part D enhancement in 2026; $13B total, 3.5 times PDP demonstration subsidies. KFF, 2026-06-11.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-58"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"98% or more of individual MA plans offer vision (99%), dental (98%), hearing (98%) in 2026; scope varies and dental often has an annual dollar cap.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-59"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"OTC allowance offered by 66% of individual plans in 2026, down from 73% (enrollee share 68% vs 79%; SNPs 94% of plans). No usage data for OTC exists (see log 72).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-60"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Roughly 40-60% of MA enrollees use dental services in a year (MedPAC review of a dozen-plus studies; latest: Commonwealth 2023, 42%).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-61"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"2021 MCBS: 55% of non-dual enrollees with plan dental coverage had a visit vs one-third of duals.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-62"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Non-dual MA enrollees with plan dental coverage: dental spending $892 in 2022; share paid out of pocket fell from 61% (2017) to 35% (2022). MedPAC's pooled visit rate is 56% for 2017-2022 (the engine's \"57% in 2022\" not located in extracted text).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-63"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"More than three-quarters of MA plans used a dental benefit limit of at least $1,500 in 2023 (Nasseh et al.).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-64"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"About 8% of MA enrollees use hearing services in a year (surveys).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-65"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"\"hearing-aid use is 1-2%\" → median plan encounter-submission rates, which MedPAC did not treat as use rates.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-66"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Share of conventional-plan enrollees with hearing exam + aid coverage rose from about 41% to 92% (2014-2025).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-67"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"FFS Medicare does not cover hearing aids or fitting exams.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-68"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"40-60% of MA enrollees use vision services in a year (Commonwealth survey: 41%).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-69"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"MedPAC did not measure utilization; says Medicare's data are insufficient for most supplemental benefits.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-70"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"For the newer extras (OTC, meals, transport, rent): encounter data are inadequate to assess use at all.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-71"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Cai et al. 2025 (JAMA Netw Open; MEPS + MCBS 2017-2021; 76,557 NON-dual beneficiaries; cross-sectional): MA enrollees did not get more dental, vision or hearing care than TM (hearing-aid wearing 13.4% in both, per the paper). Read in PMC, not just the press release.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-72"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"MA paid 9.0% less for eyeglasses ($205.86 vs $226.12) and 9.3% less per dental visit; no less for most other services.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-73"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Only about 54% of MA enrollees said they had dental or vision coverage from their plan (a survey answer, read by the authors as awareness).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-74"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Not in the engine's report: MA enrollees still paid 57.9% of their dental/vision/hearing costs themselves ($9.2B a year); plans paid 24.5% ($3.9B), a figure the authors say overstates plan spending by about $520M (hearing-aid proxy).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-75"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Cai et al. is from critics of MA payment (co-authors include Woolhandler and Himmelstein; the lead author says MA's extra money \"is not justified\" in the press release). Weight the finding, name the stance; the data are public surveys and the design is cross-sectional.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-76"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"CMS: 2027 MA enrollment projected 34 million, \"approximately 47.4% of all people enrolled in Medicare\". MedPAC's 55% is of MA-ELIGIBLE beneficiaries (Part A and B), a different denominator. Pass C/D.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-77"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Rural (pass C's subgroup): Park et al. 2025 (HSR; MCBS 2017-2021, cross-sectional) found rural MA enrollees had the most trouble paying medical bills: 11.4% vs rural TM 9.4%.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-78"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"ATI defines MA enrollment as at least one month in MA or related managed care (incl. Medicare-Medicaid Plans, Cost Plans, PACE), so its MA group is not \"MA plans you can buy\".","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-79"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Annals conclusion verbatim: small differences, no consistent pattern (the right air phrasing: \"no clear saving\", not \"MA costs more\").","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-80"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"The engine's summary: \"In 2019, 13% of sampled prior-authorization denials met Medicare coverage rules.\" A share of DENIALS, in a sample.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-81"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"The 13% is a point estimate (see the corrections table).","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-82"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"The engine's summary: \"In 2024, 95% of appealed skilled-nursing denials were overturned.\" The data month is June 2024; it is the insurers overturning their own denials.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-83"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"MA costs \"show up when you get sick: prior authorization, narrow networks\". Supported for post-acute care specifically (OIG and PSI).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-84"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"\"Whether MA's lower use of care is efficiency or denial.\" Both sides have peer-reviewed support; the efficiency side is stronger than the engine said (see Corrections).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-85"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"\"The Original Medicare side of the access comparison is missing entirely.\" KFF holds it (see Corrections).","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-86"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"OIG 2022: denied PA requests that met the rules \"likely would have been approved\" under Original Medicare.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-87"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"OIG 2022 sample: 250 PA and 250 payment denials from 15 large MAOs, June 1-7, 2019.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-88"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"The 15 insurers covered nearly 80% of MA enrollees (the engine had this \"per secondary summary\": it is in the primary).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-89"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"OIG 2022: 18% of PAYMENT denials met the rules; a separate stream from the 13%.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-90"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Cause 1: MAOs used clinical criteria not in Medicare rules (e.g., an x-ray before an MRI).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-91"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Only 2.8% of PA denials both met the rules and were reversed within 3 months, so most rule-meeting denials stood.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-92"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"OIG 2026 SNF: 19 MAOs, June 2024, 12% of SNF admission requests denied.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-93"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"SNF denial rates varied from 23% to 0.4% by MAO.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-94"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"18% of SNF denials were appealed.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-95"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"95% = 2,313 of 2,445 appeals.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-96"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"The 82% never appealed: OIG lists what may have happened to them (lower level of care, paid out of pocket, or no post-acute care).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-97"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"The engine: \"95% of appealed skilled-nursing denials were overturned\", given as a rate across the insurers.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-98"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Smaller MAOs' combined overturn rate.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-99"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"NaviHealth (UnitedHealth subsidiary) processed half the SNF requests and denied 14%, vs 11% in-house and 9% other contractors.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-100"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Nursing-home residents were denied SNF-level care 40% of the time vs 11% for other enrollees.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-101"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Earlier OIG (2018 report, 2014-2016 appeals): MAOs overturned about 75% of their own denials.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-102"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Why it matters in money: in Original Medicare an average SNF stay cost $16,000 in 2023 vs $6,000 for home health (OIG's context for the incentive).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-103"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"The engine reported the OIG long-term-care-hospital and rehab report only by its appeal overturn rates (36%, 43%).","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-104"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"LTCH/IRF overturns on appeal: 36% (272 of 754) and 43% (1,406 of 3,295).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-105"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"IRF overturn rates ranged 14%-86% by MAO.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-106"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"The three largest MAOs denied LTCH and IRF requests at higher rates than most peers.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-107"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"The 19 MAOs covered 86% of MA enrollment in June 2024 (29.3 million).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-108"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"KFF 2024: about 52.8M PA determinations, more than 90% fully approved.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-109"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"KFF 2024: 4.1M denied in full or part, 7.7% (6.4% in 2023).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-110"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"11.5% of denials appealed in 2024 (11.7% in 2023, 7.5% in 2019).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-111"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"80.7% of appealed denials overturned in 2024; over 8 in 10 every year.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-112"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"99% of MA enrollees face PA for some services.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-113"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"1.7 PA requests per MA enrollee in 2024.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-114"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Insurer spread: UnitedHealth had the fewest requests per enrollee and a 12.8% denial rate.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-115"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"KFF on insurers' own 2025 metrics: MA standard-request denial rate 12%. Different denominator from the 7.7%.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-116"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"2025 metrics: 67% of appealed MA denials overturned.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-117"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"2025 metrics scope: six MA insurers, 69% of MA enrollment.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-118"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Insurer range 2025: 5% (Elevance) to 17% (UnitedHealth) standard denials.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-119"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"MA has an automatic independent review if the plan upholds a denial (explains high MA overturn rates vs other markets, per OIG via KFF).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-120"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Senate PSI: UHC post-acute denial rate 8.7% (2019) to 22.7% (2022) (see Corrections).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-121"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"...while UHC's overall PA denial rate barely moved.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-122"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"PSI: UHC and CVS denied post-acute at about 3x their overall rate in 2022; Humana over 16x.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-123"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"PSI scope: the three insurers cover nearly 60% of MA enrollees.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-124"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Industry rebuttal to the June 2026 OIG reports (AHIP: denials include missing documentation and administrative reasons; BMA: data predate insurers' 2025 PA pledge). Seen only in a search summary of Becker's Payer; Becker's 403'd and jina returned nothing. CVS and Humana disputes of the PSI report likewise unread.","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-125"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"\"Only MA uses prior authorization\" is false; TM uses it for a limited set of services (see Corrections).","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-126"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"TM volume FY2024: just over 625,000 reviews (about 2 per 100 beneficiaries).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-127"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"TM denial rate on those requests: 22.9%. Higher rate, far smaller volume and a narrow service list.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-128"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"TM appeals 2022: 6.4% of denials appealed; 28.7% of first-level appeals overturned.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-129"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"WISeR: TM prior-authorization pilot, six states, 2026-2031 (see Corrections).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-130"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"WISeR vendors are paid a share of averted spending (the same incentive critics raise about MA).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-131"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"WISeR's official name (see Corrections).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-132"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"42 CFR 422.101(b)(6): MA plans may use internal coverage criteria only when Medicare's criteria are \"not fully established\".","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-133"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"The rule dates from the CY2024 final rule (CMS-4201-F), effective January 2024 (gap note). eCFR's amendment list shows an April 12, 2023 amendment (88 FR 22328), consistent with CMS-4201-F; the effective date itself is not in my saved text. OIG's 2022 sample predates it either way.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-134"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Original Medicare's \"any doctor who takes Medicare\" (peg hunt): 98% of non-pediatric physicians participate; about 1% formally opted out in 2024 (psychiatrists 8.1%). Participation is not the same as taking new patients.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-135"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Psychiatrists opt out most.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-136"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"AHIP (spokesperson Chris Bond, June 11, 2026): the reports ignore cost and quality problems in post-acute care.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-137"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"AHIP: the reports cannot tell administrative denials (missing paperwork, out-of-network, excluded services) from medical-necessity denials.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-138"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"OIG agrees it did not check the underlying records; it relied on the insurers' own data and asked CMS to find out why denial rates vary. AHIP's point is fair as a limit; it does not show the denials were administrative.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-139"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"AHIP: many overturns happen because providers send information missing the first time. AHIP gives no figure.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-140"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"AHIP cites an older OIG estimate of $5.7B Medicare paid inpatient rehab facilities for care that was not reasonable and necessary. That 2018 OIG report was not opened; it concerns what Medicare itself paid, so it is an argument about rehab-facility overuse, not about MA denials.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-141"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"AHIP's Inovalon comparison, including \"126% fewer 30-day readmissions\".","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-142"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"BMA (Mary Beth Donahue, June 11, 2026): the report is 2024 data; plans have since cut about 6.5 million prior authorizations; prior authorization is \"an important tool\".","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-143"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Where the 6.5M / 11% / 15% comes from: an AHIP-BCBSA survey of the plans that made the pledge, published April 7, 2026 (see Corrections).","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-144"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"AHIP's same update: services can be put back on the prior-authorization list.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-145"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"UnitedHealthcare on the OIG reports: \"a narrow and incomplete view\"; it approves the vast majority of post-acute requests; \"We do not use AI for clinical denials.\" Star Tribune (TNS), July 17, 2026.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-146"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"UnitedHealthcare: naviHealth's overturns reflect \"continued clinical evaluation\" when more information arrives.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-147"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Humana's and CVS's own statements on the 2026 OIG reports. A CVS line appears only in a search summary of Becker's (CAPTCHA, not bypassed).","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-148"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Senators Blumenthal (D) and Hawley (R) wrote to UnitedHealthcare's CEO on July 14, 2026: the OIG reports suggest \"little has changed\" since the 2024 PSI report. Bipartisan.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-149"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"UnitedHealthcare (Heather Soule): the report mischaracterizes MA and ignores CMS criteria.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-150"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"CVS Health (Phillip Blando): its feedback on errors was not addressed.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-151"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Humana (Kevin Smith): \"a partisan report laden with errors\". None of the three named a specific error in the article.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-152"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Blumenthal: traditional Medicare \"has no prior authorization\" (see Corrections).","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-153"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"AHIP's and BMA's statements on the 2024 PSI report: not located by this pass.","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-154"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"AHIP's stated position.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-155"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Strongest evidence for the industry case: MA beneficiaries got 9.2% fewer low-value services than TM in 2019 (23.1 vs 25.4 per 100), JAMA Health Forum 2022. Caveat: most authors work for Humana and the MA data are one large insurer's claims.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-156"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"The other side, peer reviewed (BMJ 2024, 2021 policies): five big MA insurers' rules would have required prior authorization for 17-33% of Part B spending; 93% of Part B drug spending at one insurer or more; the authors say PA could be aimed better at low-value services.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-157"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"The pledge (June 23, 2025): AHIP plus BCBSA plans; six commitments; across commercial, MA and Medicaid managed care; \"257 million Americans\".","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-158"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Signatories include Humana, UnitedHealthcare and CVS Health Aetna (named in the list under this line).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-159"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Commitment: cut the scope of PA, with reductions shown by January 1, 2026; each plan picks its own cuts.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-160"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Commitment: honor prior approvals for 90 days when a patient changes insurer, from January 1, 2026.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-161"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"...which MA coordinated-care plans already had to do (see Corrections).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-162"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"The clear-explanations commitment covers commercial coverage only by January 2026 (see Corrections).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-163"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Commitment: in 2027, at least 80% of electronic approvals answered in real time. Approvals only, and only those sent with all the needed documents.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-164"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Commitment: clinical denials reviewed by medical professionals, \"a standard already in place\".","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-165"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"CMS-0057-F (final rule, Jan 2024) covers MA plans: decisions within 72 hours (urgent) and 7 calendar days (standard), from 2026.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-166"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"CMS-0057-F: a specific reason for every denial from 2026; public PA metrics first due March 31, 2026; a PA API by January 1, 2027.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-167"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Gold-carding: AHIP's own 2022 survey (26 plans, commercial enrollment only): 58% of plans used it; 73% reported some negative outcomes. AHIP opposes \"blanket mandates\".","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-168"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Insurers' own descriptions (AHIP page): Humana begins gold-carding in 2026; UnitedHealthcare's gold card covers 514 codes. Self-reported.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-169"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"CMS-4214-P implements the REAL Health Providers Act (CAA 2026 sec. 6220), from contract year 2028.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-170"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Statute named.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-171"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Verify every 90 days; hospitals/facilities at most every 12 months.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-172"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"The 12-month exception covers acute inpatient hospitals, ICUs and surgical services only.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-173"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Remove departed providers within 5 business days.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-174"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Comments due December 15, 2026.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-175"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"The plan runs its own accuracy analysis and reports the score to CMS.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-176"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"\"publish an accuracy score ... from contract year 2028\" (see Corrections). Public posting is CY2029.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-177"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Third-party certification is only under consideration.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-178"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Letter grades A-F include an E (50-59%).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-179"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Cost-sharing protection when a wrong listing sends you out of network, from CY2028.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-180"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"CMS release wording: verify every 90 days and \"report an accuracy score to CMS\".","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-181"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"OIG (Oct 2025): on average 55% of listed MA behavioral-health providers gave no service to any enrollee in 2023.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-182"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Scope: 33 MA and 19 Medicaid plans, 10 counties (of 40 MA plans reviewed overall).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-183"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"72% of surveyed inactive providers should not have been listed.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-184"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Survey size: 247 responses (from 331 sampled).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-185"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Provider types: psychiatrists, psychologists, clinical social workers only.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-186"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Senate Finance majority staff 2023: 120 calls, appointments 18% of the time.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-187"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"18% counts matching-service routings as successes; 13% without (see Corrections).","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-188"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"\"MA directories had not been audited since 2018\" (as of May 2023, staff's statement).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-189"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Fairview stops scheduling UHC MA patients from Jan 1, 2027; emergency care unaffected.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-190"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Fairview sued UHC in federal court over 2027 materials listing it in network. The filing date \"Oct. 1\" is not stated in the MPR text I saved (article dated Oct 7).","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-191"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"About 33,000 Fairview patients in UHC MA. UHC says it is negotiating \"in good faith\"; air both.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-192"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Becker's counts 25 health systems dropping or narrowing MA in 2026. Seen only via 24/7 Wall St; Becker's tracker 403'd.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-193"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"42 CFR 422.116: 90% of beneficiaries in large-metro and metro counties must be within the time and distance limit for each provider type.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-194"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"85% in micro, rural and CEAC (under 10 people per square mile) counties.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-195"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Base limits (Table 1, one cell per line in the saved text): primary care 10 min/5 mi large metro, 40/30 rural, 70/60 CEAC; medical/surgical oncology 20/10 large metro, 75/60 rural, 110/100 CEAC; acute hospitals 20/10 large metro, 75/60 rural, 110/100 CEAC.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-196"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"A plan earns a 10-point credit toward the percentage if it adds telehealth providers for listed specialties (also 10 points in certificate-of-need states).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-197"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Outpatient dialysis has no time and distance test: the facility-type list (acute hospitals through outpatient behavioral health) has no dialysis entry, and the word \"dialysis\" appears 0 times in today's eCFR section.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-198"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Why: the CY2021 rule (June 2, 2020), issued as the Cures Act let people with kidney failure join MA from 2021, waived the test for dialysis clinics and asks plans to attest instead. CRS, January 11, 2021.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-199"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Opponents' objection: clinics serve about 90% of Medicare dialysis patients. CMS's answer: plans must still give access \"with reasonable promptness\" and arrange out-of-network care if the network falls short.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-200"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Realized access to dialysis for MA enrollees (travel, clinic exclusions, outcomes): no study located by this pass.","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-201"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"KFF (June 20, 2016; 2015 networks of 409 plans in 20 counties): 41% of plans left out their county's NCI-designated cancer center. Ten years old and urban-heavy.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-202"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Hu et al., JAMA Network Open 2026 (SEER-Medicare 2016-2019, 807,580 MA enrollees with 8 cancers): MA enrollees used about 12% of the oncology practices, 7% of medical/surgical oncologists and 12% of radiation oncologists that TM patients in the same county used.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-203"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Emory's summary of Hu et al.: \"fewer than one in four people on MA (23.4%)\" had recorded visits to an NCI comprehensive cancer center.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-204"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"The \"effective network\" is the care people actually received, so it mixes plan design with patient choice and distance; the authors say so. A plan has a median of 16 cancer patients but is compared with a whole county's TM patients, which by itself pushes the ratio down (this pass's note, not the authors').","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-205"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Limits: 2016-2019 SEER counties, when fewer people were in MA; no outcomes measured.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-206"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Agarwal et al. 2021 systematic review, 48 studies: MA better on preventive visits, admissions, ED visits, lengths of stay.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-207"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"...but no trend on readmissions or mortality.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-208"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"...with a selection-bias warning.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-209"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Newhouse et al. 2019: the MA mortality gap shrinks markedly over five years.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-210"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"\"The healthier start drives much of it\": the authors say MA enrollees \"become sicker relative\" over time and the standard design is flawed; \"drives much of the raw gap\" is the engine's gloss.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-211"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Landon et al. JAMA 2022, heart attack: MA lower 30-day mortality in 2009 (STEMI 19.1% vs 20.6%).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-212"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"...no significant difference by 2018 (STEMI 17.7% vs 17.8%).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-213"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"...MA readmissions consistently lower (2009 STEMI 13.8% vs 15.2%).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-214"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Roy et al. JAMA Netw Open 2025: 6.3 fewer SNF days, 3.6 fewer home-health days in MA. Design: 2021 vs 2015 cross-temporal difference-in-differences, not a trial.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-215"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"...1.5-point lower readmission, 0.3-point lower mortality, and the authors' conclusion \"not associated with worse outcomes\".","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-216"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Huckfeldt et al. 2024 (Ohio retirees moved to TM): inpatient rehab up 9.7 points, no change in 30-day mortality or readmissions.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-217"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"The NHATS figures the engine credited to the Ohio retiree study belong to a different paper: fewer MA enrollees reported functional improvement, 63.1% vs 71.7% (see Corrections).","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-218"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Same study: self-reported, 2,357 users of post-acute care, NHATS 2015-17; no significant difference in improvement among those who had finished care.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-219"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Jung, Carlin, Feldman 2025: 12.8-17.5% lower resource use in MA, 2016-2019. Estimates depend on coding-intensity adjustment.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-220"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"MedPAC calls the 12-16% range \"consistent with its own 15-18% efficiency estimate\". MedPAC text not opened by this pass (domain D).","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-221"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"188 of 508 MA-PD contracts (37%) at 4+ stars for 2027.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-222"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Enrollment-weighted average 3.99, down from 4.01. The values sit in Table 1's last row (2024 4.07, 2025 3.95, 2026 4.01, 2027 3.99); note 2025 was lower than 2027.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-223"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"About 71% of MA-PD enrollees in 4+ star contracts. The table implies about 67% for 2026 (229 of 516 contracts, 44%) (see Corrections).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-224"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"PDP enrollee share at 4+ rose from 2% to about 22%.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-225"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"What stars measure: up to 43 measures for MA-PD contracts (KFF says \"about 40\": cancer screenings, flu shots, chronic-care services, members' ratings, customer service).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-226"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"2027 stars set 2028 bonus payments.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-227"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Quality bonuses at least $13.4B in 2026.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-228"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"2.3% of projected MA payments ($574B).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-229"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"68% of MA enrollees in bonus-eligible plans in 2026, down from 75%.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-230"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"UnitedHealth gets 29% ($3.9B).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-231"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"MedPAC's measure is about $16B. KFF calls its own $13.4B a lower bound.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-232"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Bonus mechanics: 5-point benchmark increase, 10 in \"double bonus\" counties.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-233"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Stars are assigned per contract, not per plan.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-234"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Higher-rated contracts had larger disparities, 2015-16 data.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-235"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Star-rating disparities: r = 0.2 between ratings simulated for low- and high-income enrollees in the same contract (2015-16 data) (see Corrections).","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-236"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Alignment sued in D.C. district court after the 2027 release; 4+ share of members fell from 98% to 25%.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-237"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Alignment: more than $200M in 2028 payments at stake.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-238"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Clover won a ruling on 20 measures, \"May 27, 2026\". Healthcare Dive says \"this spring\"; the exact date is not in my saved text.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-239"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"\"CMS voluntarily recalculated the 2026 ratings only for plans that would gain\". Healthcare Dive says CMS recalculated without the first 10 measures for MA insurers and withdrew the second 10 only for Clover; \"only for plans that would gain\" is not in this source.","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-240"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"KFF: the recalculation adds about $600M to 2027 federal payments; and KFF projects star changes add $18.6B over 10 years. Neither KFF piece was opened.","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-241"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"\"Critics, including MedPAC, say the stars may be a weak quality signal\". MedPAC's own text not opened; the PMC paper is an academic critique, not MedPAC.","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-242"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"CMS recalculated the 2026 stars for all plans and revised only those that rose (see Corrections).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-243"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"KFF: about $600M more in 2027 bonus payments (brief updated August 12, 2026).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-244"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"UnitedHealth Group gains most, then Clover, BCBS of Alabama, Humana, CVS.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-245"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"\"KFF projects that CMS's star-rating changes will add $18.6B over 10 years\" (the engine).","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-246"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Those measure removals start with the 2029 star ratings.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-247"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Clover ruling: May 27, 2026, S.D. Georgia; 20 measures; Clover's main PPO went from 3.5 to 4.5 stars (resolves hand pass B, log 239). Seyfarth Shaw client note, a law-firm secondary.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-248"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"CMS's June 17, 2026 notice: voluntary recalculation of 2027 bonus ratings, all Part D measures removed; CMS says it is not a permanent policy.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-249"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"MedPAC (March 2026): the stars do not reliably measure MA quality (the engine's \"critics, including MedPAC\" is confirmed).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-250"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"MedPAC's reasons: too many measures (close to 100 collected, over 40 scored), contract-level reporting across wide or non-contiguous areas, \"cliff effects\".","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-251"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"MedPAC: the program pays rewards only, no penalties, about $16B in 2026; in June 2020 it recommended replacing it with a value-incentive program.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-252"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"MedPAC's 2026 counts differ from KFF's: 40% of rated contracts and 64% of enrollees in bonus status (KFF: 68% of enrollees), average 3.98 (the 2027 fact sheet's table says 4.01 for 2026). Different bases and dates; never mix them.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-253"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"The non-renewal SEP runs Dec 8 to the last day of the following February.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-254"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Effective dates Jan 1 / Feb 1 / Mar 1 by month of request.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-255"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"The SEP \"overlaps the AEP only on the Dec 8 start\". The AEP ends Dec 7; no overlap.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-256"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Medicare.gov describes the non-renewal window the same way.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-257"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Someone who picks nothing is placed in Original Medicare.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-258"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"A non-renewing plan must mail every enrollee at least 90 days ahead (about Oct 2, 2026 for a Jan 1 end; date is arithmetic).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-259"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Plans must tell members leaving to Original Medicare about their Medigap guaranteed-issue rights.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-260"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Trial right case 1: dropped Medigap to join MA for the first time, and leaves within 12 months.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-261"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"... within the first 12 months.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-262"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Trial right case 2: joined MA at first eligibility at 65, leaves within 12 months.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-263"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Case 1 gets back the same Medigap policy if the same insurer still sells it (not a free pick).","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-264"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Case 2 (joined MA at 65) can buy any Medigap policy.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-265"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"The plan-ends / leaves-area right; guaranteed issue bars pricing by health, not price in general.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-266"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"The Medigap window for the 2027 plan endings runs \"about Nov 1, 2026 to Mar 4, 2027\" (the engine): the start date.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-267"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Medicare.gov's version of the plan-leaving window: 60 days before to 63 days after.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-268"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"The right only applies if you go to Original Medicare, not another MA plan.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-269"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Which plans: Medicare.gov lists A, B, C, D, F, G, K, L for the plan-leaving cases (the line continues \"C, D, F, G\" then \"K, or L\").","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-270"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"C and F are closed to anyone who turned 65 on or after Jan 1, 2020.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-271"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Outside the windows there is no federal guarantee, and a policy may cost more.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-272"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Only four states require continuous or annual guaranteed issue for everyone 65+. (KFF, published 2026-10-13.)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-273"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Maine's annual window covers at least Plan A only.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-274"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Massachusetts requires Feb-Mar, but all insurers offer year-round.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-275"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"DE, IL, MN, RI have narrower protections.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-276"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Rhode Island's 2025 law gives MA enrollees an annual guaranteed-issue window during open enrollment. Not in the engine's report.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-277"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Delaware's 2025 windows for MA enrollees allow underwriting-based pricing and a pre-existing wait.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-278"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Illinois's year-round access is one insurer's practice, not law.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-279"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Maine extends the federal MA trial right to three years. Not in the engine's report.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-280"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Birthday rules are in \"CA, OR, IL\". KFF lists sixteen states.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-281"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Illinois's birthday rule is current-carrier-or-affiliate only, ages 65-75 (NAIC chart as of May 11, 2026).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-282"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Outside the windows, federal law allows denial or higher premiums.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-283"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"74% of full-year 2023 MA enrollees, about 20.9 million, had a condition that could trigger a denial. Exposure, not denials.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-284"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"The true share may be higher.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-285"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Coding intensity may inflate it; chart-review records were excluded.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-286"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Gen Re 2024: 33% of applications underwritten; 76% of those approved and placed, 15% declined. Voluntary survey: 39 questionnaires covering 87 companies; no rated-up or former-MA breakout.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-287"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Last-year-of-life MA enrollees left for traditional Medicare at more than twice the rate (2016).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-288"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Same, 2017.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-289"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Duals nearly three times; one insurer nearly 10 times (2017).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-290"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Traditional Medicare paid $422M (2016) and $490M (2017) more than MA would have been paid.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-291"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"CMS has implemented GAO's monitoring recommendation; its own 2019-2021 analysis still found elevated exits in certain contracts.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-292"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"35 of 126 high-disenrollment contracts showed health-biased disenrollment (2014 data).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-293"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Poor-health enrollees 47% more likely to leave those contracts; leavers cited providers and access. \"Disenroll\" here means leaving the contract, to any coverage.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-294"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Medicare spent 27% ($2,585) more on MA leavers than similar always-traditional-Medicare people, 2022 (KFF, 2024-12-06; comparison group: continuously traditional Medicare, health-adjusted).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-295"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"After a cancer diagnosis, switching to traditional Medicare rose 2.1% → 4.7% in NY, CT, MA vs flat elsewhere (Kwon et al., JAMA Health Forum 2025; SEER-Medicare, age 69+, 2014-2019).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-296"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"A 2.5-point difference-in-differences; placebo on duals found nothing.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-297"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"2026 JAMA Health Forum (Meiselbach): new complex conditions → more MA exits, more so in Medigap-protection states. Direction confirmed from JAMA's own release.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-298"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Same study's numbers: +3.3 points any new condition; +1.5 points in the four guaranteed-issue states (2016-2021 data). Secondary only; the article itself was not opened.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-299"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Long-stay nursing-home residents: conventional MA → traditional Medicare fell from 22.0% to 11.6% (2010-11 to 2021-22, risk-adjusted).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-300"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"D-SNPs 11.5-16.0%; I-SNPs 3.4-5.2%.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-301"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"MA is 55% of eligibles (Feb 2026, MedPAC; KFF March 2026 also 55%).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-302"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Trend: 19% (2007) → 55% (2026); growth slowing (3% in 2026); CBO projects 63% by 2034.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-303"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"CMS's 2027 projection of 47.4% uses all Medicare enrollees and plan projections CMS says run low.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-304"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Guaranteed-issue states do not show lower MA share except Massachusetts (39%); CT 62, ME 63, NY 58, CA 56, OR 58, IL 48 in the same table.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-305"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"2019-20 switching 6.8% TM→MA vs 2.0% MA→TM; duals 11.2% vs 4.5% (Unuigbe et al., JAMA Health Forum 2022). Rates use different denominators (each is a share of its origin program).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-306"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Switching's share of new MA enrollment growth, 49% → 67%.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-307"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"21% of MA vs 6% of traditional-Medicare enrollees switched in two years (Commonwealth Fund, survey Nov 2023-Jan 2024).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-308"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"49% of switches were MA-to-MA; 17% vs 5% said a broker convinced them.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-309"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Overall satisfaction does not differ between the programs.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-310"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"53% of large employers using MA for retirees give at least some no choice (2024); MA use up from 26% (2017) to 56%.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-311"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Rural (new): rural MA enrollees switched to traditional Medicare at about twice the nonrural rate (MCBS 2010-16). Abstract only.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-312"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Rural (new): the guaranteed-issue exit effect after cancer was larger in nonmetro areas.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-313"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Commonwealth Fund: duals report higher satisfaction in MA, \"a genuine point in Advantage's favor for low-income people\" (the engine, from a TechTarget summary).","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-314"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"The 62% vs 45% figures themselves. Not in the saved page text (chart only); TechTarget not opened.","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-315"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Medigap framing does not apply to duals: insurers generally cannot sell them Medigap.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-316"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Duals in MA: 28% of full-benefit duals in MA are in general plans, not D-SNPs; 23% of MA enrollees are in SNPs (2026).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-317"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Under-65 (disabled) enrollees: the federal MA trial right does not apply to them.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-318"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"\"80% of new MA enrollees in 2022 were switchers\" (Health Affairs via risehealth.org). Not opened.","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-319"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Meiselbach 2026 Medigap-state effect of +1.5 points (four states). Secondary only.","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-320"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"MedPAC says the excess is not profit.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-321"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"The engine: the extra money funds the extras (rebates), \"not that it is profit\".","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-322"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Traditional-Medicare enrollees pay for the extras through Part B.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-323"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"\"No CBO, GAO or independent estimate adjudicates.\" CBO does (see log 402). No GAO estimate was opened in this pass.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-324"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Plans bid to deliver the A/B benefit at or below FFS cost (MedPAC 95% after adjustment).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-325"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Nearly 100% of plans bid below benchmark. 2026: 5 of 5,555 bids above.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-326"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Medicare spends 14% more for MA enrollees than FFS would for the same people, a projected $76B in 2026.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-327"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"That is 12% of total MA payments of about $615B (2026).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-328"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Favorable selection is about 11% above FFS, $57B of the $76B (2026).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-329"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Definition: people whose risk scores overpredict their spending enroll in MA more often. MedPAC lists several possible causes (propensity to use care, unmeasured health, benefit design); \"healthier\" is one of them, not the definition.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-330"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"MedPAC calls its selection estimate robust (its own claim; the industry disputes it, log 413–428).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-331"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"After CMS's 5.9% cut, MA risk scores are about 4% higher than FFS, worth $22B (2026).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-332"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Before the cut, about 14% higher in 2024 and about 10% in 2026.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-333"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"V28 cuts coding intensity 2.9 points a year, 2024-2026; CMS has never gone above the legal minimum cut.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-334"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"$57B + $22B exceed $76B because before both adjustments MA would be about $3B below FFS.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-335"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Last year MedPAC projected 20% (about $84B) for 2025, so \"the gap fell from 20% to 14%\". True as published, but the method changed (ESRD added, coding data revised); recomputed, 2025 is about 117%.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-336"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"(same) the recomputed earlier years, as read by an industry coalition (HLC); the chapter's Figure 12-4 shows 117% for 2025.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-337"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"The 2025 projection was $84B.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-338"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"The 2025 split was nearly even: $44B selection, $40B coding. (see Corrections)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-339"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"2026 estimate newly includes ESRD enrollees.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-340"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Unadjusted, 2026 bids are 83% of FFS, benchmarks 107%, payments about 99%.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-341"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Medical costs alone about 18% below FFS; 13% of bids are admin and profit (2026).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-342"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"After adjustment, benchmarks are 124% of what FFS would spend on the same people.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-343"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Quality bonuses: MedPAC about $16B in 2026; KFF at least $13.4B (KFF's is a lower bound assuming average risk scores).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-344"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"KFF: at least $13.4B in 2026, up from $12.7B; 68% of MA enrollees in bonus plans, down from 75%. (MedPAC counts 64%; different bases.)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-345"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"UnitedHealth gets $3.9B (29%) of bonus spending.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-346"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Part B premiums are about $11B higher in 2026 because of higher MA payments.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-347"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"About $175 a year, $14.61 a month, per beneficiary (every Part B payer, MA or FFS; a MedPAC estimate, not a line on anyone's bill).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-348"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Rebates average $2,660 per beneficiary per year in 2026 (all plans, conventional and SNP).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-349"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"More than doubled since 2018; about 15% of payments.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-350"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Total capitated payments average $16,242 per beneficiary (2026).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-351"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Conventional plans' Part D enhancement $50 and non-Medicare $51 PMPM. Swapped: Part D $51, non-Medicare $50.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-352"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"\"$2,660 average ... plans project spending about 35% on lower cost sharing, 22% non-Medicare, 26% Part D, 9% Part B, 8% admin/profit\". The split is conventional plans only, whose rebate is about $2,391 a year; plans keep $199 of it for admin and profit.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-353"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Conventional split with admin separated out: 35 / 22 / 26 / 9 / 8.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-354"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"The chapter summary's own split (26/38/19/7/10) is a different population (all non-employer plans incl. SNPs). (see Corrections)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-355"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Rebate share is as low as 50% but typically 65% or 70% depending on stars. CBO gives 50-70%.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-356"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Rebates reached a record $222 PMPM (all non-employer plans) in 2026, from $209 in 2025.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-357"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Conventional plans rose from $95 PMPM (2018) to $197 (2023), $188 (2025), $199 (2026).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-358"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"MA organizations actually spent about $24B on non-Medicare extras in 2023, the first actual-spending figure; use is still unknown.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-359"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Individual MA-PDs put more than $600 per enrollee (over $50 a month) of rebates into Part D in 2026; about $13B, 3.5 times the PDP stabilization subsidy ($3.6B).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-360"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"The engine's claim: \"KFF: about $924 per enrollee per year in 2015 vs nearly $2,400 in 2026\". It is the average rebate in individual plans, per MedPAC, not extra spending. (see Corrections)","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-361"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Current equivalent, MedPAC 2026: conventional plans (non-employer, non-SNP) about $199 PMPM, which is about $2,388 a year and matches KFF's \"nearly $2,400\". All non-employer plans: $222 PMPM (hand pass D, log 357).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-362"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"KFF (2026-07-01): $28B of the 2026 $76B is coding. MedPAC: $28B is 2025, $22B is 2026 (log 364). (see Corrections)","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-363"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"MedPAC (March 2026): coding added $28B in 2025 and $22B in 2026.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-364"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"MedPAC: coding intensity totals $233B of extra payments, 2007-2026 ($184B through 2024).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-365"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"The per-enrollee overpayment (not the rebate): MedPAC 2024, $2,329 per MA enrollee, of which $2,142 went to extra benefits and $187 to plan retention (admin and profit). Quoted by MedPAC's defenders; MedPAC's 2024 report was not opened.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-366"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"The 2027 payment increase: +2.48%, over $13B.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-367"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"4.98% including risk-score trend (CMS expects coding trend alone to lift scores 2.50%).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-368"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Up from 0.09% in the January advance notice; \"about $700 million\" is \"over $700 million\".","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-369"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"CMS kept the 2024 risk model instead of the proposed update (the main reason the cut shrank from -3.32% to -1.12%).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-370"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Chart-review exclusion −1.53%; −1.78% without the switcher exception; audio-only 0% (the advance notice's −1.53% was a different policy: log 372).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-371"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"\"Sources of diagnoses stayed at -1.53%\" in both versions, read as the same policy. The advance figure covered all unlinked chart reviews under the proposed model.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-372"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Net savings to the Trust Funds $6.84B in 2027, net of the government's share of the bid-benchmark gap and Part B premiums.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-373"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Plans may still submit the diagnoses; they just stop counting.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-374"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Rationale is the face-to-face requirement.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-375"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"CMS declined to delay or phase in. CMS answers the delay requests by restating the policy and promising technical guidance; it does not use the word \"decline\".","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-376"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"The statutory minimum coding cut stays at 5.90% for 2027.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-377"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"2025-05-21: audit all eligible contracts each year (about 550, up from about 60).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-378"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Coders from 40 to about 2,000.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-379"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"\"about $17B a year\" overbilling per federal estimates. CMS's wording; the release names no source.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-380"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"\"MedPAC estimate of up to $43B\". Not a current MedPAC figure (2026 coding: $22B).","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-381"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Last significant recovery was from the PY2007 audit.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-382"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"No PY2018-2024 results posted; PY2018 shows audited-contract lists only. (see Corrections)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-383"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"2023 rule projected about $479M a year from PY2018, $4.7B over 2023-2032. (Law-firm summary of the rule.)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-384"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"2025-09-25 N.D. Tex. vacated the rule on notice-and-comment grounds; CMS appealed 2025-11-21.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-385"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Fifth Circuit argued 2026-10-05; panel \"seemed poised to toss the rule out, again\" (a reporter's read; no ruling found).","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-386"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Jan 2026 memo: PY2020-2024 audits about every three months from Feb 2026; samples 35-200.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-387"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"\"CMS will comply with the vacatur, so no extrapolation for now.\" Not in the Crowell memo summary (no mention of extrapolation).","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-388"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"UnitedHealth's Q3 2026 release mentions the DOJ only as a risk factor.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-389"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Independent Health to pay up to $98M (guaranteed $34.5M); DxID's ex-CEO $2M; no admission. The $63.5M contingent figure is not in this source.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-390"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"CMS said PY2018 findings would start in mid-2026. None are posted as of 2026-10-21 (hand pass D, log 383). The date has slipped. (see Corrections)","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-391"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"CMS memo 2026-01-27: audits are years behind.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-392"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Same memo: PY2011-2013 audits found 5-8% overpayment rates; recoveries \"will begin soon\" (no recovery yet, as of January 2026).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-393"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"CMS posted the PY2011-2013 overpayment lists on 2026-03-04.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-394"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Summed from the posted lists: PY2011 $3,952,086.49; PY2012 $5,715,759.39; PY2013 $3,321,977.13 (30 contracts each). These are identified, not shown as collected. (Same column header in the PY2012 and PY2013 files.) (see Corrections)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-395"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Those amounts are sample-only (PY2012 method: the sum over sampled enrollees, no extrapolation). (see Corrections)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-396"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"CMS memo: HHS will comply with the vacatur while it stands and keep auditing. It does not say \"no extrapolation\". (see Corrections)","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-397"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"The appeal: No. 25-11293, from N.D. Tex. 4:23-CV-909.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-398"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"The engine's claim: argued 2026-10-05. (see Corrections)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-399"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Georgetown's \"Briefing is ongoing\" label is stale (the case was argued the day before the page update). (see Corrections)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-400"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"The engine's claim: no ruling yet. The court's opinions feed (2026-10-14 to 10-20) lists no Humana opinion and the opinion path 404s; 10-05 to 10-13 is unchecked. (see Corrections)","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-401"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"\"No CBO, GAO or independent estimate adjudicates\" the payment gap (the engine): CBO, July 2026.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-402"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"CBO: after the 5.9% cut, MA risk scores about 4% higher (agrees with MedPAC's 2026 4%).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-403"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"CBO's 5-10% is raw per-beneficiary spending, different populations. (see Corrections)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-404"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"CBO on selection: savings from selection may not reach the government because plans may not bid them away.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-405"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"GAO-12-51 (January 2012): before CMS's adjustment, 2010 MA risk scores were 4.8-7.1% higher than in FFS for the same people.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-406"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"GAO-13-206 (January 2013): CMS's 3.4% cut was too small; excess payments of $3.2-5.1B over 2010-2012.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-407"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"GAO-13-206: 2012 coding pushed scores 4.9-6.4% higher.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-408"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"GAO's status note on GAO-12-51: CMS (December 2025) says 5.9% has been sufficient 2019-2026. (see Corrections)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-409"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"...but CMS has shown GAO no analysis; the recommendation stays \"Open - Partially Addressed\". (see Corrections)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-410"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"GAO-16-76 (April 2016): CMS's national audit estimated $14.1B improper MA payments in 2013, mainly unsupported diagnoses.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-411"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"GAO-16-76: CMS's RADV contract selection missed the riskiest contracts (PY2011 audit).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-412"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"AHIP (2024-01-26): selection may reflect access barriers, not health.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-413"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Nearly 15% of FFS is Part A-only; limiting to A+B would raise FFS spending 6% (2021).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-414"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"MedPAC's answer: the Part A-only fix moves FFS spending about 1 point, not 6 (2021 post), and in 2026 it found \"no systematic effect\".","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-415"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"AHIP: MedPAC assumes selection and coding are additive without justification.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-416"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Humana: the coding measure leans on the AAPCC model (R-squared under 1%).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-417"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Humana: survivorship bias (decedents removed from MA but not FFS).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-418"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Humana: extrapolating from under half of MA \"effectively doubles\" selection, nearly 5 points on 2024.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-419"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Humana's own study: selection 4.9-5.8% (2020-2022), 42-65% below the 10-15% \"often cited\". Written by a Humana subsidiary; the journal article itself was not opened.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-420"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"FTI: 44% of new 2022 beneficiaries went straight into MA, so FFS-only data miss them; also care delay and same-county rules. No funder is disclosed on the page.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-421"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"AHIP: CMS \"effectively rebutted\" MedPAC by keeping the 5.9% minimum. AHIP's characterization; CMS kept the minimum again for 2027, and CBO says every published study finds the minimum does not fully correct coding.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-422"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Paragon: Milliman (for UnitedHealth) put MA payments about 96% of FFS; none of the industry studies estimate coding, selection or spillovers.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-423"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Paragon: bids cover the basic benefit at roughly 80% of FFS. MedPAC 2026: 83% raw, 95% adjusted (see Corrections).","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-424"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Milliman's study was commissioned by UnitedHealth (2024-04-29).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-425"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"BMA (2026-09-03): MedPAC leaves out Part D and Medicaid and the value of the cap and extras.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-426"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"BMA: \"CMS officials\" found a 1.5–2% coding difference, against MedPAC's \"roughly 10%\" (a different measure: MedPAC's figure after CMS's cut is about 4%; see Corrections).","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-427"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"HLC: selection method \"discounts 62% of the MA population\" (industry coalition reading of MedPAC).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-428"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"The critique: two current and two former MedPAC commissioners challenged MedPAC's estimate (Forefront, June 2024). Their article was behind Cloudflare and is known here only through its opponents' account.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-429"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"The critics, by name.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-430"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Critics' points as reported by the rebuttal: including employer-group (EGWP) enrollees in the selection estimate breaks \"analytical norms\"; a non-peer-reviewed comparator; ESRD left out; \"overpayment\" vs \"differential payment\".","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-431"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"The defence (Lieberman, Ginsburg, Berwick, Gilfillan; Forefront 2024-07-01): the critics' cited study is about upcoding, not selection; MedPAC cites over a dozen peer-reviewed selection studies.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-432"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"The defenders run their own selection research. Lieberman and Ginsburg (Forefront 2023-06-13) argued selection could exceed MedPAC's then-$27B (6%) estimate, using switchers at open enrollment 2006-2019.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-433"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Health Affairs Forefront itself was not read (Cloudflare check to curl and to r.jina.ai; not bypassed).","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-434"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"The WSJ reported in May 2025 that the DOJ was running a criminal investigation of UnitedHealth's Medicare business (press report of a press report; WSJ itself is paywalled and was not opened).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-435"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"On 2025-07-24 UnitedHealth told the SEC (Form 8-K) it was complying with formal criminal and civil requests from the DOJ.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-436"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Same 8-K: UnitedHealth's own framing that the special master found \"no evidence\" of wrongdoing. This is the company's paraphrase; the recommendation is not a final ruling (log 443–446).","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-437"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"The phrase \"criminal and civil\" appears in only one UnitedHealth SEC filing between 2025-06-01 and 2026-10-21: that 8-K (SEC full-text search, one hit).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-438"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"The latest 10-Q (Q2 2026, filed 2026-08-10) does not mention a criminal probe (the word \"criminal\" appears zero times); it says only that the company responds to investigations generally, including on risk-adjustment coding. No charge, settlement or closure is disclosed.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-439"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Same 10-Q: the company's reviews include risk-adjustment coding compliance.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-440"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Poehling began as a 2011 sealed whistleblower suit; the DOJ joined in part in February 2017.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-441"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"What the DOJ alleges (an allegation): about $2.1B less would have been paid had unsupported codes been deleted (2009-2016 chart reviews).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-442"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"KFF Health News: the special master recommended granting UnitedHealth's \"motion to dismiss\". It was a summary-judgment recommendation (log 444). (see Corrections)","v":"Corrected before air","r":""},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"March 2025, the special master recommended summary judgment for UnitedHealth on all remaining claims; the DOJ asked the court to reject it in April 2025.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-444"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"The special master's reasoning (Suzanne Segal, appointed by Judge Olguin).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-445"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"The government's motion for review was argued 2025-11-19 and taken under submission; no public ruling since. Latest docket activity 2026-10-08 is a sealed document (CourtListener notes its RECAP copy \"may not be up to date\"). (see Corrections)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-446"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"UnitedHealth's own read: the outcome cannot be estimated (Q2 2026).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-447"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Georgetown's tracker labels Poehling \"Briefing is ongoing\" (page updated 2026-09-13). The label is loose: briefing ended in 2025 and the motion is under submission (log 446).","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-448"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"The federal Medigap open enrollment period: six months, starting with the first month a person is both 65 or older and enrolled in Part B (42 U.S.C. 1395ss(s)(2)(A)).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-449"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"What the window guarantees, in the statute: no denial, no conditions, no pricing by health status.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-450"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"CMS's own Medigap guide (March 2026 edition) says the same in plain words.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-451"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"It happens once; it does not come back each year.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-452"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"During it: any policy sold in the state, and no extra charge for health problems.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-453"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Even inside the window, a policy may exclude a pre-existing condition for its first six months (six-month look-back); six months of prior creditable coverage removes the wait. (see Corrections)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-454"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Price can still vary by age, smoking, sex and area, even in the window. (see Corrections)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-455"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"The clock is set by Part B sign-up and cannot be moved or repeated (delaying Part B while on current-employer coverage delays it).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-456"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"After it ends there is no federal guarantee of a sale; an insurer may underwrite and refuse unless a guaranteed-issue right applies (log 261–272).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-457"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"After it ends, the price can also be higher because of health.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-458"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"As of 2026-10-21 the 2027 Part B premium is not announced. SSA's 2027 COLA release (2026-10-14) says the amount reaches enrollees through the my Social Security Message Center \"starting in late November\"; the CMS 2027 fact-sheet URL patterns returned 404 the same day. (see Corrections)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-459"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Precedent for timing: CMS released the 2026 figures on November 14, 2025.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-460"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"2026 Part B standard premium: $202.90 a month (log 45).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-461"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"2026 Part B deductible: $283.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-462"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"2026 Part A hospital deductible: $1,736 per benefit period (log 40).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-463"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"2027 Part B premium: the trustees' estimate is $209.50 a month. A projection: \"expected about $210\", never the announced figure.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-464"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"2027 Part B deductible: the trustees' intermediate estimate is $292 (log 46).","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-465"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"2027 Part A hospital deductible: the trustees' intermediate estimate is $1,788 (Table V.E1). Not announced either.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-466"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Both routes pay the Part B premium (2027 figure pending, log 459, 464).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-467"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"2027 Part D out-of-pocket threshold $2,400 (from $2,100), deductible $700 (log 55, 56).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-468"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"2027 Advantage in-network out-of-pocket ceiling: $9,850 (log 34).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-469"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Anyone new to Medicare since 2020 cannot buy a Medigap plan that pays the Part B deductible; Plan G is Plan F's benefits minus that deductible, so the Original-plus-G route still pays it.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-470"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"High-deductible Plan G, 2027: $3,050 before the policy pays (the one 2027 Medigap figure already published).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-471"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Plan G premium: the 2025 average among current policyholders of all ages, $194 a month ($2,323 a year), $164 (New Mexico) to $257 (New York) (log 48). Not a 2027 figure and not a 65-year-old's quote. (see Corrections)","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-472"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"The 2026 Medigap guide's Plan K/L footnote calls $202.90 the \"yearly Part B deductible\". It is the 2026 Part B premium; the deductible is $283 (log 462). (see Corrections)","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-473"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"The regulation sets the Advantage in-network cap on basic benefits only (42 CFR 422.100(f)(4)).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-474"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"\"Basic benefits\" means Part A and Part B items and services, minus hospice (and kidney-transplant organ acquisition); Part D drugs are not in it (42 CFR 422.100(c)(1)).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-475"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"CMS's 2027 bid memo: the in-network and PPO combined caps are for Parts A and B services; the 2027 PPO combined range tops out at $14,800. HMOs generally have no out-of-network cap.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-476"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"The Advantage cap does not apply to Part D, which has its own separate cap ($2,100 in 2026; $2,400 in 2027, log 468).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-477"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Two separate caps, 2027, in network: $9,850 + $2,400 = $12,250 (our arithmetic), excluding premiums, out-of-network care in an HMO, extras and non-covered drugs; a PPO's worst case is $14,800 + $2,400 = $17,200 before premiums. That premiums sit outside the cap is an inference from log 474. (see Corrections)","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-478"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"The Advantage open enrollment period: an Advantage member may make one change from January 1 to March 31, to another Advantage plan or out to Original Medicare (with a drug plan) (42 CFR 422.62(a)(3)).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-479"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Medicare.gov: the January–March window is only for people already in an Advantage plan; it cannot be used to move from Original Medicare into Advantage.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-480"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"The January–March window carries no Medigap guaranteed-issue right of its own; outside the six-month window and the listed situations (the nearest is the first-year trial right), a Medigap insurer may refuse. (see Corrections)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-481"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"The trial right, as KFF summarises the federal rule: leave Advantage within the first year and you can buy any Medigap policy sold in your state.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-482"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"A \"Part B giveback\": an Advantage plan uses part of its rebate to pay down the member's Part B premium.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-483"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"By plans, 2026: 32% of individual Advantage plans offer some Part B reduction; among them 36% give $100 a month or more and 28% give $10 or less. (see Corrections)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-484"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"By enrollees, 2026: 31% are in a plan that reduces the Part B premium, most often by under $10 a month; among individual-plan enrollees getting one, 39% get under $10 and about 32% get $100 or more.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-485"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"2027: the giveback cannot exceed the 2026 Part B premium ($202.90). No 2027 share-of-plans figure is in this record (KFF's 2027 spotlight returned 404).","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-486"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"An average Part B giveback amount (2026 or 2027).","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-487"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"A provider leaving an Advantage network mid-year: the plan must tell members, for cause or not. Primary care and behavioral health: written notice plus one phone attempt at least 45 days before, to anyone seen in the past three years; other specialists: written notice at least 30 days before, to patients seen regularly (42 CFR 422.111(e)).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-488"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"A switch window opens only when CMS judges a network change significant: affected members may move to another Advantage plan or to Original Medicare, once per change, from the month they are notified to two calendar months later. (see Corrections)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-489"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Medicare.gov: that window is decided case by case.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-490"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Hospice for an Advantage member, in the regulation: the member stays in the plan, but Original Medicare pays the hospice (and other covered services); the plan's payment drops to its rebate share plus any Part D payment (42 CFR 422.320).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-491"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Medicare.gov: once hospice starts, Original Medicare covers the terminal illness even for someone who stays in an Advantage plan; the plan keeps covering unrelated care and extras while premiums are paid.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-492"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"The hospice carve-in test is over: VBID's hospice component ended at the end of 2024 and the whole VBID model at the end of 2025; no hospice carve-in found in the CY2027 final rule or rate announcement, so hospice is still carved out for 2027 on this record.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-493"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"QMB (Qualified Medicare Beneficiary) pays the Part B premium (and Part A if not premium-free) plus Medicare deductibles, coinsurance and copays. 2026 federal limits: $1,350 a month income and $9,950 resources for one person; $1,824 and $14,910 for a couple (slightly higher in Alaska and Hawaii).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-494"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Under QMB, Medicare providers may not bill the person for Medicare cost sharing (a small Medicaid copay may apply); QMB members also get Extra Help (no more than $12.65 per covered drug in 2026).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-495"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"SLMB pays the Part B premium only. 2026 limits: $1,616 a month for one person, $2,184 for a couple; resources $9,950 / $14,910.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-496"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"QI pays the Part B premium. 2026 limits: $1,816 a month for one person, $2,455 for a couple; same resource limits. Reapply every year; states approve first come, first served.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-497"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"The limits are federal floors: states may be more generous, and people apply through their state, not Medicare.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-498"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Extra Help (the Part D Low-Income Subsidy), 2026 limits: income $23,940 a year and resources $18,090 for one person; $32,460 and $36,100 for a couple. Automatic with full Medicaid, a Medicare Savings Program or SSI. For 2027: plan premium $0, deductible $0, up to $5.80 per generic and $14.40 per brand-name drug, $0 after $2,400 in total drug costs.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-499"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"People with both Medicare and Medicaid: nearly 12.5 million in FY2025 (an average over the year; nearly 14 million enrolled at some point); 72% with full Medicaid benefits, 28% partial (help with Medicare premiums and cost sharing). (see Corrections)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-500"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"MedPAC/MACPAC (CY2022 data): 13.6 million dually eligible in at least one month; 51% of them were enrolled only in managed care for Medicare, against 40% of other beneficiaries.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-501"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"D-SNPs: about a quarter of full-benefit duals were in plans that align Medicare and Medicaid in 2025 (over 2 million people). Log 317 stands (3.9 million full-benefit duals in Advantage in 2026, 28% of them in plans open to everyone).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-502"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"The Medicare Savings Program gap: of people eligible in 2018–2020, 56.7% were enrolled, so about 2 in 5 were not; take-up ranged from 41.5% (Ohio) to 72.9% (California); those not enrolled were healthier, with more income and assets.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-503"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"The Extra Help gap: in 2020, 3.6 million people were potentially eligible but not getting it (2.9 million already in Part D, about 661,000 with no drug coverage). Pre-dates the 2024 expansion: the latest official count, not a current one.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-504"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Savings Program take-up was higher among eligible people in Advantage than in Original Medicare, 61.3% against 52.9%. Cross-sectional: it does not show Advantage causes enrollment.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-505"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Full-benefit duals in D-SNPs against Original Medicare (MCBS 2015–2019, adjusted including for age): better reported access, preventive care and satisfaction in D-SNPs, including satisfaction with out-of-pocket costs +4.8 points against Original Medicare and +5.9 against other Advantage plans; few differences against other Advantage plans overall; the authors say D-SNPs have not been consistently better. Satisfaction and access, not spending or health outcomes. (see Corrections)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-506"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Low-income people not on Medicaid: the existing evidence stands. 12% of Advantage against 24% of fee-for-service enrollees spent over 20% of income on care, in an Advantage group that is poorer (log 15–17); against it, the Annals switcher study's larger burden estimates below 200% of poverty (log 21). Nothing new settles it.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-507"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"A Medigap premium as a share of a low income: a 2021 opinion piece puts Plan G at \"6% to over 60%\" of income for one person at 150% of poverty, citing a consumer website for the premium range. No current named source gives the share. Off air.","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-508"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"SHIPs give free personalized counselling and are not tied to any insurer.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-509"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"What SHIP is: a federal grant program to states for Medicare counselling, outreach and information.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-510"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"SHIP funding: just over $55 million in federal discretionary money for FY2026, flat for four years; some states add their own.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-511"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Who counsels: trained counsellors, often state or local agency staff, plus trained volunteers; one-on-one help includes plan comparisons.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-512"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Where: every state, DC, Guam, Puerto Rico and the US Virgin Islands; find yours at shiphelp.org or the state list in Medicare & You 2027.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-513"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"1-800-MEDICARE (1-800-633-4227; TTY 1-877-486-2048) helps compare plans and costs, around the clock.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-514"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"The Plan Finder (Medicare.gov/plan-compare) compares health and drug plans on premiums, costs and benefits.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-515"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"The Plan Finder checks networks: add your important providers and the results show whether each plan has them in network; the handbook also says to contact the provider directly.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-516"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"The Plan Finder checks drugs: add your prescriptions and pharmacies to compare plans; drug costs depend on the formulary and tier.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-517"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Senior Medicare Patrol: ACL-managed grants funded from the federal Health Care Fraud and Abuse Control program (about $35.2 million in 2023); staff and trained volunteers help people prevent, detect and report fraud, errors and abuse, in every state, DC and the territories. It is not plan-choice counselling.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-518"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"How to reach Senior Medicare Patrol: smpresource.org or 1-877-808-2468.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-519"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"2027 CMS limits on broker pay per Advantage enrollment: $725 national for a new enrollment ($816 in CT/PA/DC, $902 in CA/NJ, $495 in PR/USVI); stand-alone Part D $130; referral fee cap $100.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-520"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"2027 renewal (each later year the person stays): $363 national; Part D $65. These are ceilings, not what every plan pays.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-521"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"2026 limits: $694 initial, $347 renewal (national); Part D $114 / $57. The 2027 cap rose about 4.5% (our arithmetic).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-522"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Why a broker isn't neutral, in CMS's own words (CY2025 rule, April 2024): plans and their marketing middlemen structured separate \"administrative\" payments that get around the caps. CMS's finding, not a court's.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-523"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"CMS said it had seen ads offering agents bonuses and perks for enrollments, booked as add-ons outside the caps. An allegation in CMS's preamble about ads it saw, not a measured share of plans.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-524"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Court status: a federal court in the Northern District of Texas stayed the CY2025 compensation changes on July 3, 2024 and vacated them on August 18, 2025 (CMS's account). (see Corrections)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-525"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"The government did not appeal, so the pre-2025 rules apply for 2027: add-on payments for services other than enrollment sit outside the enrollment cap, limited only to their market value. (see Corrections)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-526"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"The pre-2025 add-on rule: training, customer service, recruitment, overhead or health-risk-assessment help may be paid separately, up to marketplace value.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-527"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"CMS's 2027 memo asks plans to report voluntarily when they pay $0 to independent agents for a plan, citing concerns, \"including those expressed by several states\", about broker pay during the 2026 enrollment period.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-528"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"What those 2026 concerns were (trade press: plans cutting commissions to $0 on some plans). The memo names no plan and describes no conduct; no primary in this record states it.","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-529"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"CMS's own count of marketing complaints: 15,497 in 2020; 39,617 in 2021 excluding December. Dated, and not a like-for-like year. (see Corrections)","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-530"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Senate Finance majority staff (one party's staff) collected complaint information from 14 states and reported, as examples it heard, agents telling seniors their doctors were in a new plan's network when they were not. Allegations from state complaint files, not adjudicated findings.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-531"},{"s":"ep44-medicare-advantage-or-original","n":44,"c":"Nine of the ten states that tracked numbers reported rising complaints.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-532"}]);